      "my_answer": "Carpal tunnel syndrome (CTS)",
      "rationale": "The patient has classic symptoms of nocturnal paresthesias in the median nerve distribution, relief with shaking the hand (‘flick sign’), positive Tinel and Phalen signs, sensory deficits, thenar atrophy, and grip weakness—all consistent with median nerve compression at the wrist."
    },
    {
      "id": "Q2",
      "question": "Is this condition urgent, and does it require emergent intervention?",
      "my_answer": "Requires non-emergent care",
      "rationale": "While there is evidence of motor involvement (thenar atrophy, weakness), which suggests moderate severity, there is no acute thenar paralysis or profound motor deficit indicating imminent irreversible damage, so emergent surgical decompression is not immediately required."
